Tim Starr had an interesting story about giving feedback to their local 
government.  He and the local police chief were friends and so could more 
easily discuss goings on with the police activities.

> When government is small, such feedback through personal relationships is
possible.

I will add to his story with one of my own.

I was entering a resturant with some friends when I noticed someone who 
worked in my apartment complex and a man in a wheelchair "talking" with a 
police officer.  When I next saw him, I asked him about the incident.  He 
said that his father (in the wheelchair) had challenged the police officer 
for parking in a handicapped spot.  The officer responded that he was on 
"official business" and besides he would not be there long.  I got the 
impression that this exchange was tense and not very polite.  His father got 
the name and badge number of the officer.  His father later called the 
officer's superior to complain about this behavior.  It turns out that the 
superior's son is confined to a wheelchair!  I would have loved to have heard 
what the superior said to officer after that!

So, you need not have a personal relationship with local government officials 
or live in a small community (I live in Phoenix) to give feedback, but sure 
it helps.

