Subject: EXT: Kids vs. Uploading

Hal Finney:
> Nick makes the point that competitive pressures may force our uploaded
> selves to cut back on "human-style" luxuries like virtual eating and
> sex in order to keep up with our more streamlined competitors. 

To elaborate on this, I suspect that sex and eating might be radically
altered rather than done away with entirely.  For example we could
still have orgasm, but it might be elicited by making a copy of
oneself, or expanding one's memory capacity, rather than by sensual
stimulation.  This would rejoin the link between motivation and
propagation that is the driving force of evolution.  Hunger for food 
might be replaced with hunger for knowledge.  For some of us that change 
might be useful even before uploading!

The analogy to implanting an ant's brain in a human is a good one, 
but the most important changes will be in quality, not quantity.  
Of course, which changes are best may indeed be largely a matter of 
taste (and vocal debate!), but it will also be a matter of evolution,
and we can to some extent anticipate now what the most successful
uploaders will choose.

