From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: VOTE: better ways?

Anton,
  Thanks for your informative posting on the bidding method for fair division.
You must be psychic -- I half-finished a post about a method for deciding
group choices by adding up individual utilities, but was having trouble
doing away with the need for an impartial utility estimator.

The method you discuss is useful for doing just that.  The key
to the method is the incentive for each participant to accurately
estimate the worth he/she places on each item.

However, you may have noticed that participants in this method only
have a _partial_ incentive to be accurate in their bids.  I'm
interested in figuring out just how this incentive works.

For instance, if I'm certain that I'm going to lose the bidding
on the car (say, if I know that you'll bid $1500) then it's to my 
advantage to inflate my bid in order to get more compensation from you.
I must weigh my risk of getting stuck with the car against my profit
from over-bidding.  If your bid is revealed to me beforehand, then
my risk goes to zero, and I should bid exactly $1 below the highest bid
for each item I don't wish to buy, and $1 above the highest bid for each
item I want.

I wonder if we can come up with a modified method that allows public
bids and still encourages accurate bidding?


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

