This is going to depend on exactly what you mean by a concept of murder.
The Yanamamo sure recognize homicide as meriting revenge.  If I remember
Chagnon right, the constraints on homicide aren't that different from the
constraints on theft--if you can get away with it it just shows how tough
you are, but trying it is likely to get you in big (fatal, in the case
of homicide) trouble with your victim or his relatives.
     In fact, doesn't Chagnon describe a whole spectrum of ritualized
violence prescribed for intra-community quarrels, all designed to
let quarrels be settled violently without anyone being killed?  I guess 
it's not clear whether the point of avoiding killing is moral or
practical (i.e. to prevent feuds that would destroy the community)--is
this the distinction you were getting at? 
     Now, killing someone from outside the community isn't stigmatized at
all, but that's a pattern found in lots of cultures--"murder" is only
killing one of your own kind, and the definition of "your own kind"
can be pretty narrow.
 
> I suspect that other pre-agricultural societies that engage in
> sporadic but continuous warfare are the same.  There's probably enough
> information on the New Guinea highlanders to check them.

Just for the record, the Yanamamo, the New Guinea highlanders, and
most other head-hunting or warlike cultures I know of are sedentary
agriculturalists, not pre-agricultural.


