Date: Wed, 24 Feb 1993 16:50-EST
From: Marc.Ringuette@GS80.SP.CS.CMU.EDU
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

Robin Hanson writes,
> On the log vs linear return thread, I have to say it's intuitively
> obvious that log is better - but it would take a bit of thinking to
> remember what convinced me of this....

The log measure is a very useful one, but I wish I didn't have to
depend on it so much.  The problem with it is, I really do care about
expected dollars earned in a lot of cases, not just their logarithm.

I've been thinking about this recently in the context of card-counting
in Blackjack (a positive-expectation high-variance game).  Over in
rec.gambling, there's a lot of talk of the Kelly Criterion, which does
what Robin suggests and maximizes log growth of your bankroll.  I
believe this is optimal for limited bankrolls and infinitely long
investment sessions.  It is the assumption of an infinitely long
session that I object to, however:  it makes the log-growth method too
conservative.

---

How about if you make a wildly different set of assumptions for playing a
positive-expectation game:  infinite bankroll, limited number of betting
rounds, and a goal of maximizing expected profit?  Then you should bet the
maximum allowed at each step.  This is the other extreme.

---

Slightly less extreme:  fixed bankroll, limited number of betting rounds,
goal of maximizing expected profit.  This sounds like the case that was
discussed earlier in the "double or half" game.  In this case you should 
still bet the maximum possible at each step, because maximizing your
expectation for one betting round also maximizes your expected profit
for future rounds.

---

So how can we find an intermediate case where your goal is still
expected profit rather than expected log(profit), but where you don't
bet the maximum all the time?

Here's one attempt.  Assume a fixed bankroll, a limited-length betting
session, and a MAXIMUM TAKE-HOME AMOUNT after which you must quit betting.
This puts a cap on how wild the up-swings can be.  This may seem arbitrary,
but I find it interesting.  It's closer to my real case than the log-bankroll
assumption, because I really do care about dollars profit, not just the
logarithm, as long as the amounts don't get too large.

What's my optimal strategy under these assumptions?  One thing we know about
the strategy is that it's going to maximize my _expected_action_ subject
to the constraints.  This is because our profit in the game is, by
assumption, a fixed fraction of each dollar bet.  Bet more dollars,
make more money (on average).

Let's calculate the exact strategy by working backwards from the end of the
trip.  On the final bet of the week, I should bet max(b,TAKEHOME-b), where b
is my bankroll at that point and TAKEHOME is my maximum take-home amount.  We
can work backwards, doing a case-by-case analysis, to figure out how much I
should bet for all bankrolls b and all numbers of bets remaining n.  This
yields a dynamic programming solution for figuring out how much to bet in a
given situation.

---

I haven't got much farther than that, but at least it's an _attempt_ to
deal with the problem of limited-length betting sessions and realistic
bankrolls.


-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

