From: drw@BOURBAKI.MIT.EDU
Subject: LAW: custom vs first principles

   From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)

   So, you are telling me that in Yanamamo society there is NO social
   sanction for killing your neighbor whatsoever? I find this not
   merely hard but impossible to believe, and I suspect that it is up
   to you to demonstrate that this is the fact rather than for us to
   go off and read your reference.  I'm calling "burden of proof"
   here.

Pardon me if I'm amused.  You've told me to go read the literature
quite a number of times.  I find it hilarious that you aren't willing
to take the medicine you hand out.

The basic references are:  Napoleon Chagnon, "Yanamamo, the fierce
people".

1st ed.: 1968, F2319.2.Y25.C433
2nd ed.: 1977, F2520.1.Y3.C5

These are not exhaustive anthropological studies, but summaries of the
culture for students.  I've read them both and remember them both
pretty well, and although it's not stated as such, it's pretty clear
that in Yanamamo society, killing someone is not considered an
"offense"; there is nothing "wrong" with it, as there is, say, with
incest.  Of course, a relative of the victim might get mad at you and
kill you, but that's strictly a private matter.  The Yanamamo seem to
feel no need to produce a justification for killing someone, although
they always have an explanation (of course).

The Yanamamo seem to be one of they very few "primitive" cultures that
were reached by the anthropologists before they were pacified by
colonists.  The colonists, coming from market-based societies, see one
of their principle purposes the suppression of warfare, as warfare
hurts trade.  Thus, most cultures studied by anthropologists have had
their internal warfare suppressed by a colonists for a generation or
more before they were studied.  The Yanamamo (at the time Chagnon
studied them) were still engaging in continuous skirmish warfare,
although there is some indication that their internal warfare has been
intensified by the effects of indirect contact with industrial
culture.

Interestingly, the Yanamamo are also a "pre-market" type of culture;
there are very few quid-pro-quo transactions.  There are a lot of
cases where resources are distributed based on kinship, and
gift-exchange relationships are common, and some cases where favors
are returned, but there are no explicitly quid-pro-quo trades, as far
as I can tell.

What little I know of the New Guinea highlanders matches this
description also.

It's likely that before the development of the first "states" (about
4000 BC in Mesopotamia), all of human culture was like this.

Dale

