From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: EXT: Selective breeding

> From
> what little comparative information I've seen, the birth rate in
> various countries is far more correlated with the social conditions
> than the availability of modern birth control.  

Well, I've seen quite a bit of the data, and I'd say birth rate
is very strongly correlated with availability, convenience, and ability
to use birth control, more than any other factor you could name.
Furthermore, the cause and effect is quite clear, which is not
true for most of the "other social conditions."  I don't think 
there is a more open and shut case in epidemiology, but there does
seem to be a strong resistance to facing the fact that we have, for
the first time, radically and irrevocably short-circuited 
the most important part of genetic evolution, cutting off
motivation from result, with the resulting radical alteration
of reproductive rate and behavior.

As for infanticide, genes over the many millions of years since primates
first started practicing infanticide have developed a counter-motvation,
infant-mother bonding.   There is no such counter-motivation to birth
control, and no hundreds to thousands of generations it would take
for it to emerge from genetic selection.

Infanticide, tree bark, etc. were effectively proscribed in many 
cultures, and were not nearly as effective as modern birth control, 
which in many subpopulations has lowered the birth rate far below the 
replacement level of 2.2 children/lifetime (closer to 1.5
children/lifetime in college educated women, for example).   
Any high-tech colony will genocide itself, unless some strong
combination of extropian techniques and motivations for boundless
expansion take over the meme pool.

