>    
> >Any high-tech colony will genocide itself, unless some strong
> >combination of extropian techniques and motivations for boundless
> >expansion take over the meme pool.
> >
> 
> But I find this assertion hard to believe.

Come again?  Self-genocide follows directly from having a birthrate well 
below the replacement level.  Elsewhere I have argued that the birthrate 
will continue to fall to around 1.0 children/lifetime: many of 
those 1.5 are still accidental, as birth control still has plenty of 
room for improvement.  The higher tech the colony, the more demand
there will be for career women, and less desire to want or be
a "baby factory", and the lower the birth rate will go.  But 1.5 still 
reduces the population to zero over a longer period of time.  So space 
colonies, ocean colonies, or over the long run high-tech civilizations 
in general are self-genociding, under today's cross-cultural assumptions, 
from Japan to Silicon Valley to Germany.  

Increased wealth has led to _decreased_ birthrate, although education, 
desire for career and above all the functionality of birth control are more 
important demographic factors than wealth.  The "I can't afford more 
children" argument actually means "I can't afford to raise children to 
expectations", which have increased faster than the level of wealth,
until the standard expectation in some circles has now become that
we can't afford any children at all, because the world is "overpopulated".  
Despite these supposed "limits", the folks in Africa, Bangladesh, etc. can 
"afford" to have 6 children/lifetime, despite earning only 1% of what we do.

Some combination of rapid and continuous increase in lifespan, longer 
fertility span, and higher birthrate could bring the high-tech colony 
back up above ZPG; a spectacularly successful extropian memeset might
go further and bring about something resembling "boundless expansion".
That now requires a conscious choice, ie extropian values; genetic 
instinct has been short-circuited and can no longer be relied upon to 
motivate expansion.  We must now engineer our own motivations, or die.

