From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

I've been thinking of a case where it would be rational to take
a high risk investment, even if the expected return is negative.

Take the case of a lottery, where the payoff is $600,000 and
the chance of winning is 1 in 1,000,000 for a $1 ticket.  A 25-year
old earning $10/hr has to invest 0.1 hr to buy the ticket.  If
he wins, he can avoid working for the rest of his life.  Let us
say that he would work 40 years.  At 2000 hr/year that's 80,000 hr.
So, measured in time, his expected return is 0.08 hr.  If he
spends less than the interest income each year, he can pass on
the payoff to his children.  Let us say that he values leisure
for his children at half of the value of leisure for himslef.  Then
his total expected payoff is 0.12 hr.  Now his lottery ticket
is a rational purchase from a time point of view.

